# PART 3 — MULTIPLE PARTIAL DELIVERIES AGAINST THE SAME PO

This is the most important part of the business workflow.

After a PO has been registered, the administrator must be able to prepare one or more deliveries over time.

For example:

- Customer PO contains 12 accepted quotation lines.
- First delivery: select the items currently available.
- Second delivery: return to the SAME PO and select additional available items.
- Third delivery: deliver the remaining items when they become available.

Every delivery must remain linked to the same customer PO.

## Delivery preparation UI

Implement this workflow within the existing order detail interface.

When an administrator opens an order, provide a clear action:

`Prepare a New Delivery`

Open a professional modal or panel showing the accepted PO lines.

For every order line, display:

- Original item number.
- Description.
- Unit.
- Ordered quantity.
- Already delivered quantity.
- Quantity reserved in other active delivery notes.
- Quantity still available for a new delivery.
- Selection checkbox.
- Quantity to deliver input.

The administrator must only be able to select items that still have an available quantity.

The administrator may deliver:

- A complete remaining item quantity.
- A partial quantity of an item.
- Multiple items together.
- Any subset of the accepted PO lines.

Validation rules:

- Delivery quantity must be greater than zero.
- Delivery quantity must not exceed the remaining available quantity.
- At least one item must be selected.
- Never allow a quantity to be delivered twice.
- Never allow total delivery quantity to exceed the accepted PO quantity.

## IMPORTANT: Prevent duplicate allocation

Use the existing delivery tables.

A line can already be allocated to another delivery note that is PREPARED or IN_TRANSIT, even if it has not physically been delivered yet.

Therefore:

`Available quantity = Ordered quantity - Quantity allocated to all non-cancelled delivery notes`

Also display physical delivery progress separately:

`Delivered quantity = Sum of quantities belonging to delivery notes with status DELIVERED`

A cancelled delivery note must release its allocated quantities.

This distinction is essential to prevent two administrators from assigning the same stock quantity to two different deliveries.

The server/database must enforce these rules. Do not rely only on frontend validation.

Use atomic database operations and appropriate concurrency protection so that two users preparing deliveries simultaneously cannot over-allocate the same order line.

